Task 1: Use ipa-healthcheck for Diagnostics
Subtask 1.1: Install and Configure ipa-healthcheck
The ipa-healthcheck tool provides comprehensive health monitoring for IdM installations.

Install the ipa-healthcheck package:
sudo dnf install -y freeipa-healthcheck
Verify the installation:
rpm -qa | grep healthcheck
Check the default configuration:
sudo cat /etc/ipahealthcheck/ipahealthcheck.conf
Subtask 1.2: Run Basic Health Checks
Execute a complete health check:
sudo ipa-healthcheck
Run health check with verbose output:
sudo ipa-healthcheck --verbose
Generate health check report in JSON format:
sudo ipa-healthcheck --output-type json > /tmp/idm-healthcheck.json
View the formatted JSON report:
python3 -m json.tool /tmp/idm-healthcheck.json | less
Subtask 1.3: Analyze Specific Health Check Categories
Check only certificate-related issues:
sudo ipa-healthcheck --source ipahealthcheck.ipa.certs
Check DNS configuration:
sudo ipa-healthcheck --source ipahealthcheck.ipa.dns
Check replication status:
sudo ipa-healthcheck --source ipahealthcheck.ds.replication
Create a script to run regular health checks:
sudo tee /usr/local/bin/idm-health-monitor.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
# IdM Health Check Monitor Script

LOGFILE="/var/log/idm-healthcheck.log"
TIMESTAMP=$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')

echo "[$TIMESTAMP] Starting IdM Health Check" >> $LOGFILE

# Run health check and capture results
/usr/bin/ipa-healthcheck --output-type json > /tmp/healthcheck-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).json

# Check for critical issues
CRITICAL_COUNT=$(ipa-healthcheck --output-type json | jq '[.[] | select(.result == "CRITICAL")] | length')

if [ "$CRITICAL_COUNT" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "[$TIMESTAMP] ALERT: $CRITICAL_COUNT critical issues found!" >> $LOGFILE
    # Send alert (could integrate with monitoring system)
fi

echo "[$TIMESTAMP] Health check completed" >> $LOGFILE
EOF
Make the script executable:
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/idm-health-monitor.sh
Task 2: Check IdM Logs in /var/log/dirsrv and krb5kdc.log
Subtask 2.1: Explore Directory Server Logs
Navigate to the Directory Server log directory:
cd /var/log/dirsrv
ls -la
Identify your IdM instance directory:
sudo find /var/log/dirsrv -name "slapd-*" -type d
View the main Directory Server access log:
sudo tail -f /var/log/dirsrv/slapd-$(hostname -s | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')/access
Check Directory Server error logs:
sudo tail -20 /var/log/dirsrv/slapd-$(hostname -s | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')/errors
Monitor real-time LDAP operations:
sudo tail -f /var/log/dirsrv/slapd-$(hostname -s | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')/access | grep -E "(BIND|SEARCH|ADD|MOD|DEL)"
Subtask 2.2: Analyze Kerberos KDC Logs
Locate and examine the Kerberos KDC log:
sudo tail -20 /var/log/krb5kdc.log
Monitor authentication attempts in real-time:
sudo tail -f /var/log/krb5kdc.log
Search for failed authentication attempts:
sudo grep -i "failed" /var/log/krb5kdc.log | tail -10
Analyze successful ticket requests:
sudo grep -i "issue" /var/log/krb5kdc.log | tail -10
Subtask 2.3: Create Log Analysis Scripts
Create a comprehensive log analysis script:
sudo tee /usr/local/bin/idm-log-analyzer.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
# IdM Log Analysis Script

echo "=== IdM Log Analysis Report ==="
echo "Generated on: $(date)"
echo

# Find IdM instance
INSTANCE_DIR=$(find /var/log/dirsrv -name "slapd-*" -type d | head -1)
INSTANCE_NAME=$(basename $INSTANCE_DIR)

echo "=== Directory Server Statistics ==="
if [ -f "$INSTANCE_DIR/access" ]; then
    echo "Total LDAP operations today:"
    grep "$(date '+%d/%b/%Y')" $INSTANCE_DIR/access | wc -l
    
    echo "BIND operations:"
    grep "$(date '+%d/%b/%Y')" $INSTANCE_DIR/access | grep -c "BIND"
    
    echo "SEARCH operations:"
    grep "$(date '+%d/%b/%Y')" $INSTANCE_DIR/access | grep -c "SRCH"
    
    echo "Recent errors:"
    tail -5 $INSTANCE_DIR/errors
fi

echo
echo "=== Kerberos Statistics ==="
if [ -f "/var/log/krb5kdc.log" ]; then
    echo "Authentication attempts today:"
    grep "$(date '+%b %d')" /var/log/krb5kdc.log | wc -l
    
    echo "Failed authentications:"
    grep "$(date '+%b %d')" /var/log/krb5kdc.log | grep -c -i "failed"
    
    echo "Recent Kerberos activity:"
    tail -5 /var/log/krb5kdc.log
fi

echo
echo "=== System Resource Usage ==="
echo "Disk usage for logs:"
du -sh /var/log/dirsrv /var/log/krb5kdc.log 2>/dev/null

echo
echo "=== Recent Critical Events ==="
journalctl -u ipa -n 5 --no-pager
EOF
Make the script executable and run it:
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/idm-log-analyzer.sh
sudo /usr/local/bin/idm-log-analyzer.sh
Task 3: Set Up Log Aggregation with Syslog for IdM Logs
Subtask 3.1: Configure Rsyslog for IdM Log Aggregation
Create a dedicated rsyslog configuration for IdM:
sudo tee /etc/rsyslog.d/50-idm.conf << 'EOF'
# IdM Log Aggregation Configuration

# Directory Server logs
$ModLoad imfile
$InputFileName /var/log/dirsrv/slapd-*/access
$InputFileTag dirsrv-access:
$InputFileStateFile stat-dirsrv-access
$InputFileSeverity info
$InputFileFacility local0
$InputRunFileMonitor

$InputFileName /var/log/dirsrv/slapd-*/errors
$InputFileTag dirsrv-errors:
$InputFileStateFile stat-dirsrv-errors
$InputFileSeverity error
$InputFileFacility local0
$InputRunFileMonitor

# Kerberos KDC logs
$InputFileName /var/log/krb5kdc.log
$InputFileTag krb5kdc:
$InputFileStateFile stat-krb5kdc
$InputFileSeverity info
$InputFileFacility local1
$InputRunFileMonitor

# IdM aggregated log file
local0.*    /var/log/idm-dirsrv.log
local1.*    /var/log/idm-kerberos.log

# Stop processing these messages
local0.*    stop
local1.*    stop
EOF
Restart rsyslog service:
sudo systemctl restart rsyslog
sudo systemctl status rsyslog
Verify the configuration:
sudo rsyslogd -N1
Subtask 3.2: Configure Log Rotation
Create logrotate configuration for IdM aggregated logs:
sudo tee /etc/logrotate.d/idm-logs << 'EOF'
/var/log/idm-dirsrv.log /var/log/idm-kerberos.log {
    daily
    rotate 30
    compress
    delaycompress
    missingok
    notifempty
    create 0644 root root
    postrotate
        /bin/kill -HUP `cat /var/run/rsyslogd.pid 2> /dev/null` 2> /dev/null || true
    endscript
}
EOF
Test logrotate configuration:
sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/idm-logs
Subtask 3.3: Set Up Remote Log Forwarding
Configure rsyslog to forward IdM logs to a remote server (optional):
sudo tee -a /etc/rsyslog.d/50-idm.conf << 'EOF'

# Forward IdM logs to remote syslog server
# Uncomment and modify the following lines for remote logging
# *.* @@remote-log-server:514
# local0.* @@remote-log-server:514
# local1.* @@remote-log-server:514
EOF
Create a local log monitoring script:
sudo tee /usr/local/bin/idm-log-monitor.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
# IdM Log Monitoring Script

DIRSRV_LOG="/var/log/idm-dirsrv.log"
KRB5_LOG="/var/log/idm-kerberos.log"
ALERT_LOG="/var/log/idm-alerts.log"

# Monitor for critical patterns
monitor_logs() {
    # Check for authentication failures
    if tail -100 $KRB5_LOG 2>/dev/null | grep -q -i "failed"; then
        echo "$(date): WARNING - Kerberos authentication failures detected" >> $ALERT_LOG
    fi
    
    # Check for directory server errors
    if tail -100 $DIRSRV_LOG 2>/dev/null | grep -q -i "error"; then
        echo "$(date): WARNING - Directory server errors detected" >> $ALERT_LOG
    fi
    
    # Check for replication issues
    if tail -100 $DIRSRV_LOG 2>/dev/null | grep -q -i "replication"; then
        echo "$(date): INFO - Replication activity detected" >> $ALERT_LOG
    fi
}

# Run monitoring
monitor_logs

# Clean old alerts (keep last 1000 lines)
if [ -f "$ALERT_LOG" ]; then
    tail -1000 $ALERT_LOG > ${ALERT_LOG}.tmp
    mv ${ALERT_LOG}.tmp $ALERT_LOG
fi
EOF
Make the monitoring script executable:
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/idm-log-monitor.sh
Set up a cron job for regular monitoring:
sudo crontab -e
Add the following line:

*/5 * * * * /usr/local/bin/idm-log-monitor.sh
Subtask 3.4: Create a Log Dashboard Script
Create a comprehensive log dashboard:
sudo tee /usr/local/bin/idm-log-dashboard.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
# IdM Log Dashboard

clear
echo "========================================"
echo "       IdM Log Monitoring Dashboard     "
echo "========================================"
echo "Last updated: $(date)"
echo

# Function to display log statistics
show_stats() {
    local logfile=$1
    local description=$2
    
    if [ -f "$logfile" ]; then
        echo "=== $description ==="
        echo "Total lines: $(wc -l < $logfile)"
        echo "Last 5 entries:"
        tail -5 "$logfile" | sed 's/^/  /'
        echo
    else
        echo "=== $description ==="
        echo "Log file not found: $logfile"
        echo
    fi
}

# Display statistics
show_stats "/var/log/idm-dirsrv.log" "Directory Server Logs"
show_stats "/var/log/idm-kerberos.log" "Kerberos Logs"
show_stats "/var/log/idm-alerts.log" "IdM Alerts"

# Show current system status
echo "=== System Status ==="
echo "IdM Services:"
systemctl is-active ipa --quiet && echo "  IPA: Active" || echo "  IPA: Inactive"
systemctl is-active dirsrv@* --quiet && echo "  Directory Server: Active" || echo "  Directory Server: Inactive"
systemctl is-active krb5kdc --quiet && echo "  Kerberos KDC: Active" || echo "  Kerberos KDC: Inactive"

echo
echo "=== Recent Health Check Status ==="
if [ -f "/tmp/idm-healthcheck.json" ]; then
    python3 -c "
import json
try:
    with open('/tmp/idm-healthcheck.json', 'r') as f:
        data = json.load(f)
    critical = sum(1 for item in data if item.get('result') == 'CRITICAL')
    warning = sum(1 for item in data if item.get('result') == 'WARNING')
    success = sum(1 for item in data if item.get('result') == 'SUCCESS')
    print(f'  Critical: {critical}')
    print(f'  Warning: {warning}')
    print(f'  Success: {success}')
except:
    print('  Health check data not available')
"
else
    echo "  No recent health check data available"
fi

echo
echo "========================================"
EOF
Make the dashboard executable and run it:
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/idm-log-dashboard.sh
sudo /usr/local/bin/idm-log-dashboard.sh
Troubleshooting Tips
Common Issues and Solutions
Permission Denied Errors:

Ensure you're running commands with sudo when accessing log files
Check file permissions: ls -la /var/log/dirsrv/
Log Files Not Found:

Verify IdM services are running: systemctl status ipa
Check if logs are being written: ls -la /var/log/dirsrv/slapd-*/
Rsyslog Configuration Issues:

Test configuration: sudo rsyslogd -N1
Check rsyslog status: systemctl status rsyslog
Verify log file creation: ls -la /var/log/idm-*.log
Health Check Failures:

Ensure all IdM services are running
Check network connectivity and DNS resolution
Verify certificates are not expired
Verification Commands
# Verify log aggregation is working
sudo tail -f /var/log/idm-dirsrv.log &
sudo tail -f /var/log/idm-kerberos.log &

# Test authentication to generate log entries
kinit admin

# Check if logs are being captured
sudo grep "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d')" /var/log/idm-*.log
